  "fold_selection": {
    "minimax2": "Split the validation query window into an early and a late contiguous half. Score a candidate by the maximum of its two worst-metric ratios, so a candidate must improve every reported metric in both halves to score below one. Ranking and tie breaking are otherwise unchanged."
  },
  "selection_policy_rules": {
    "analogue_gate": "Admit drift candidates for a retrieval descriptor only when that descriptor's pure analogue candidate (alpha 1, beta 1, flat ramp, no shrinkage) already scores below one, that is when the retrieved analogue is itself a better validation forecast than the base trajectory. This is the validation-only form of the mechanism the object comparison established: overwriting the base trajectory can only help when that trajectory is worse than the analogue.",
    "family_prior_margin": "Admit a drift candidate only when its selection score beats the best non-drift candidate by at least the given absolute margin. A prior in favour of pure error retrieval, which is the object the method claims.",
    "conservative_tolerance": "Give up at most this fraction of the achieved validation improvement in exchange for the smallest correction magnitude alpha * (1 + beta).",
    "family_prior_relative_margin": "Admit a drift candidate only when its selection score is at most (1 - margin) times the best non-drift candidate score. The relative form of the family prior, so the required evidence scales with how much improvement is available in the cell.",
    "minimax2_and_window": "Score a candidate by the worst of three views: the whole validation window and each of its two halves.",
    "rank_stability_fraction": "Retain only candidates that rank inside the same top fraction of the admitted pool in both validation halves, so a candidate that is strong in one half and mediocre in the other cannot be selected.",
    "horizon_conditional_prior": "Apply margin_at_or_below to cells whose forecast horizon is at most the threshold and margin_above otherwise. The retrieved analogue trajectory is only trustworthy while the neighbour's future stays close to the query's; a margin of 1.0 disables the drift family entirely above the threshold. The horizon is known before any test data is read."
  },
